## HDD Connection Architecture

The Raspberry Pi 5 does not have native SATA drive connectors. Each HDD must connect through a compatible SATA-to-USB interface, unless the selected bay already includes one.

```text
                       BATTERY POWER STATION
                                |
                    +-----------+-----------+
                    |           |           |
                 Pi power    Monitor      Drive power
                    |         power          |
                    |           |        +---+---+
                    |           |        |       |
                    |           |    2.5-inch  3.5-inch
                    |           |      HDD       HDD
                    |           |        |       |
                    |           |    SATA-USB  SATA-USB
                    |           |     bridge     bridge
                    |           |        |       |
                    |           |        +---+---+
                    |           |            |
                    |           |      Powered USB hub
                    |           |            |
                    +-----------+------ Raspberry Pi 5
                                         |
                                      HDMI out
                                         |
                                  13.3-inch monitor

Bluetooth keyboard/trackpad connects wirelessly to the Pi.
```

### Left-side drive bay — 2.5-inch HDD

- Install the removable 2.5-inch SATA HDD caddy in the left-side opening.
- Connect the drive to a compatible SATA-to-USB bridge.
- Connect the bridge to the powered USB hub or an appropriate Pi USB port.
- Confirm the drive receives sufficient power during startup and normal use.
- Provide a secure mounting point and enough room to release the caddy.

### Right-side drive bay — 3.5-inch HDD

- Install the removable 3.5-inch SATA HDD caddy in the right-side opening.
- Connect the drive to a compatible SATA-to-USB bridge.
- Supply the drive with both 5V and 12V SATA power through a suitable rated power supply or dock.
- Connect the USB data interface to the powered USB hub or an appropriate Pi USB port.
- Confirm the bay, bridge, and power supply are compatible before ordering.

**Important:** The existing 12V / 2A adapter must not be assumed to power the 3.5-inch HDD by itself. Confirm whether it is part of a complete adapter/dock that also supplies the drive's 5V rail.

---

## Battery and Power Requirements

All components must operate from battery power. No component should require a wall outlet during normal use.

The initial design should use a commercial battery power station with compatible outputs and suitable power adapters. Do not choose the battery based only on its advertised wattage; verify the output profiles, total capacity, continuous output, connectors, and startup-current requirements.

| Device | Required power provision |
|---|---|
| Raspberry Pi 5 | Stable 5V USB-C supply; target 5A capability |
| HDMI monitor | 12V / 2A input |
| 2.5-inch HDD | Compatible SATA power via its bridge or hub |
| 3.5-inch HDD | Both 5V and 12V SATA power |
| Powered USB hub | Its specified external power input |
| Bluetooth keyboard | Internal rechargeable battery or its normal charging method |

The Pi 5's available downstream USB current is limited when using a lower-current power supply. A powered hub helps avoid relying on the Pi to supply all the drive power. See the official Raspberry Pi power guidance: https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/computers/raspberry-pi.html

### Existing power bank

`[OWNED]` 65W, 20,000mAh power bank.

Do not assume it can run the complete cyberdeck. Verify its supported output voltages, USB-C power profiles, simultaneous-output behavior, usable capacity, and whether it can supply the Pi and other devices together. It can remain a backup or test power source until verified.

### Battery sizing

A 250–300Wh commercial power station is an initial sizing candidate, not a confirmed requirement. Measure the assembled system's actual power consumption and account for conversion losses and HDD startup demand before deciding on a final battery.

---

## Software and Storage Configuration

- Use the included microSD card for the operating system unless a later upgrade is chosen.
- Use the Pi's USB 3 ports for the HDD interfaces where practical.
- Give each drive a stable mount name, such as `LAPTOP_HDD` and `DESKTOP_HDD`.
- Mount drives by filesystem UUID rather than relying on changing device names such as `/dev/sda`.
- Configure the operating system to mount the drives reliably at startup.
- Unmount or eject a drive in software before removing its caddy.
- Shut down the Pi cleanly before disconnecting its power source.
- Keep backups of important data; removable HDDs are not themselves a backup strategy.

---

## Case and Mechanical Layout

The final case has not yet been selected. Do not treat previous concept dimensions as final manufacturing dimensions.

The chosen case must accommodate:

- The 13.3-inch display and its mounting frame.
- The Raspberry Pi 5 and its cooling fan.
- One removable HDD bay on each opposite side.
- The full depth of the 3.5-inch drive, caddy, SATA connectors, and power connector.
- The 2.5-inch drive and its caddy.
- The battery power station or a suitably secured battery arrangement.
- The powered USB hub and cable bends.
- Power adapters, distribution, and cable routing.
- Ventilation and access for service.
- Reinforced walls around both drive-bay cutouts.

Measure the case's **internal** dimensions and check the depth behind every opening. Leave clearance for connectors, caddy release mechanisms, airflow, and mounting hardware.

